     In light of the recent couple of requests for tips, I thought I'd post
some of what I have learned.  This will include spoilers:


     I am now able to play a game and, for the most part, get my character
past the first five or six levels of the dungeon and about eight levels
of experience, each game.  I remember when I would die almost all the time
on level 4.  What changed?  I think there were some subtle changes in my
playing style that allowed me to do this.  A lot of this is probably just
intuition: Does a level 6 character commonly encounter soldier ants on
dlvl 4?  I don't have a chart; I can't answer this question yes or no,
but I *am* able to descend at a rate, most of the time, that will support
my character without running into monsters he can't handle.  Likewise,
can my level 6 Valkyrie handle a soldier ant with Excalibur?  Two soldier
ants?  At the same time?  (It helps to have a cloak of invisibility so
one can run, q.v.)
     Lately I've been playing Valkyries mostly.  They begin with a longsword.
After your character reaches level 5 (not sure on this figure) one can, with
reasonable success, get the longsword blessed and named Excalibur by dipping
it in a fountain.  This gives (and I'll say this right this time): 1d5 hit
bonus, and 1d10 damage bonus, plus protection from level-draining attacks
and automatic searching.  Good deal.  It also causes your chatracter to
consume food at a faster rate.  If you have Excalibur, take this into
account.  You should probably be "living on faith" for the most part (
don't consume food rations; pray when you are weak and your God will sate
your stomach.  Since this is usually 800+ turns plus, without a named
weapon, you normally don't get in trouble this waqy); keep track of when
you pray and make sure it's at least 700 turns.  Otherwise, eat something.
If you're out of food, stash the sword somewhere (I'm sketchy here: if you
have the weapon but do not wield it, it may or may not consume food.  I'm
not sure.  Logic would dictate that it wouldn't, but my limited experience
seems to convey the opposite impression) and get ahold of a more mediocre
weapon until you can afford the food (if you're descending in dungeon levels,
you're probably going to find enough monster corpses to handle problems).
     Some techniques:
Before reading a scroll, test it for curse (see if your pet will step on it,
or drop it on an altar).  Unless you've found destroy armor ('\' command
will verify this) take off all your valuble armor (do keep something on
that you can afford to lose, so that it *will* be identified when you do
find it).  If you can't check for a curse, wield a weapon you can afford
to have decreased in enchantment; a cursed scroll of enchant weapon can
do that.
     Before quaffing potions, test for a curse and make sure your pet is
nearby unless you've: a) discovered paralysis, and b) become telepathic.
Blindness and paralysis can kill you if you're unlucky.
     Before trying on armor, especially funny types of boots, helmets, and
gloves (not to mention cloaks) be *sure* to test for curses.  Boots or
gloves of fumbling will do horrors to your dungeoneering ability.  Reduced
chance to hit, good luck digging with a pickaxe, don't even think aBout
stealth.
     Boots of levitation are nice to have, but not nice to have cursed.
If you can get them (a cursed pair of boots is likely to be one; identify
the pair and try to uncurse it a la scroll or holy water) you can float
over an Elbereth inscription and it won't fade, regardless of your actions
on that square, just as scroll of scare monster (after a while, it should
fade on its own, however, if I understand).
     A cloak of invisibility is to be saved.
     I drop all rings I find down any available sinks, after testing them
by checking for curse and wearing them (some will be readily apparent).
They don't help if you don't know what they are, and identify scrolls don't
come readily.
     If you encounter a wand, E)nscribe something into the ground with your
fingers and then enscribe over it using the wand.  If "The engraving
disappears!" (the most common result), don't zap it on yourself; I managed
to zap myself with a wand of cancelling once (has that effect on engravings.
On you, it cancels all enchantments, thus doing horrors to your AC).  If
you can determine what it is (zapping it on a floating eye while standing
next to it is useful.  If it doesn't disappear, it's cancellation.  If it
does but you can hit it, it's make invisible.  If it shows up somewhere
else in the dungeon, it's teleport away.)  If it turns out to be make
invisible, zap it on yourself; invisibility can be inconvenient at times,
but progress offsets the liabilities and it's worth not falling prey to
mukamils and soldier ants.  Other wands have other effects.  Most notable
of these other effects is a transformation of the existing engraving:
"For a good time call Suzy at 4?0-1245" is enscribed here
isn't an uncommon message (one of the numberse always seems to be blacked
out, for obvious reasons :) ).  This is a wand of polymorph.  It's a good
idea at this point to gather up all possessions found on all levels (if
you think you can handle it, rob a few shops and kill off the Kops.  They'll
leave lots of junk behind.  You get bad karma, but that wears off after
a while.  You might want to live off the cream pies they leave behind for
a thousand moves or so in order to avert the risk of losing the favor of
your God, in this case) and zap the wand on them a few times.  A magic lamp
may be worth a wish (a la #rub, of course).... and everyone can use speed
boots, a helm of telepathy, or crystal plate mail.  Every so often you can
even end up with a named artifact, although that's never happened to me.
NOTE: Curses, plusses and charges remain constant throughout.  It's a good
idea to do this near an altar.

     I would say that the most useful single item in the game is the unicorn
horn.  You can use it to:
a) cure blindness, confusion, stunning, lost abilities (with repeated
application), sickness, and perhaps even fatal sickness (never tried).
b) clear up potions of hallucination, sickness, and I believe some
others.  If it clears up the potion, the potion was most likely not
very palatable.
c) act as a non-rustable weapon of (just from experience; I don't have stats)
similar effectiveness to a longsword.
     Given a unicorn horn, you can easily enough gain poison resistance by,
say, eating a killer bee corpse: lost strength can be regained by repeatedly
using the horn (don't be discouraged by "nothing happens.")

     I certainly hope these tips help some people.

